# Four-currency evaluation + nightly orchestrator (standing rule)

Coordinate: 29.5.10/7.6.12/4.4.7
Issued: 2026-11-08 22:26 CST
Request (verbatim): Standing rule: evaluate our daily workload in terms of disk, cpu, ram, and inference. See if a better planner could have produced better results (or faster results). Ship a new orchestrator every night. For all open tasks, you will handle the heavy lifting in terms of inference. Assume the ranch nodes have significantly better disk, memory, and cpu capacity.
Status: machinery live 2026-11-08 — rule in AGENTS.md, skill nightly-orchestrator indexed + published; v1 shipped to quarterexo@exo 2026-11-08; nightly job created (~04:44); nightly cycle continues

Notes:
- Division of labor: inference heavy lifting = Emise; disk/memory/CPU heavy lifting = ranch, on the premise that ranch nodes have significantly better disk, memory, CPU capacity than this VM (thermal/load gates still apply). Orchestrator home: ~/workspace/orchestrator/ until Will creates its repo; then Emise manages it there as part of the dreaming process. Naming question put to Will 2026-11-08 (quartermaster recommended).
- 2026-11-08 22:34: Will created the dispatcher repo and named it: wbic16/quarterexo@exo. Skill + nightly job updated to publish there; v1 builder re-briefed mid-flight to target quarterexo.

Results:
- 2026-11-08: orchestrator v1 built on Rock Mountain (cargo 1.94.1), ran clean (exit 0), published to wbic16/quarterexo@exo 0c8d1ad3 (parent f979d626), main.rs blob verified. First plan: 52 open tasks of 199 — 4 video → ranch, 17 build → ranch, 31 VM-light coordination, inference → Emise for all.
